Richard Bond Howell (October 25, 1754 – April 28, 1802) was a soldier, spy, statesman, and poet who served the new United States at every critical juncture of its founding. He fought in the Revolutionary War as an intelligence officer and combat commander, helped ratify the Constitution, governed New Jersey for eight years, and led the state&rsquo;s militia during the Whiskey Rebellion. He is one of America&rsquo;s often overlooked, unsung heroes.
Vital Records# Born October 25, 1754, Newark, New Castle County, Delaware Died April 28, 1802, Trenton, New Jersey Father Ebenezer Howell Mother Sarah S. (Bond) Howell Twin Brother Dr. Lewis B. Howell, served as Surgeon, 2nd NJ Regiment Wife Keziah (Burr) Howell, married c. 1779–1780 Buried Friends Burying Ground, South Broad Street, Trenton, NJ Children# Name Born Died Charles B. Howell 1780 1823 Mary Ann Howell 1782 1863 Sarah B. (Howell) Agnew 1783 1863 Beulah B. (Howell) Glaser 1785 Maria B. Howell 1790 1876 Richard L. Howell 1794 1841 Joseph B. Howell 1794 1874 William B. Howell 1796 1863 Franklin B. Howell 1798 1815 Early Life and the Road to Revolution# Richard Howell was born on October 25, 1754, in Newark, New Castle County, Delaware, the son of Ebenezer Howell and Sarah S. (Bond) Howell.1 He was born with a twin brother, Lewis, who would accompany him into the Revolutionary War.
In 1769, Ebenezer Howell moved the family from Delaware to Cumberland County, New Jersey. The Howells were part of a broader Welsh immigrant family with deep roots in the region; another kinsman, Rednap Howell, had already made his mark as a schoolmaster and political agitator in North Carolina. In 1773 Ebenezer built the Howell Homestead in Roadstown, and carved the initials &ldquo;RH 1773&rdquo; and &ldquo;LH&rdquo; by the rear doorway, for Richard and Lewis.
On December 22, 1774, the nineteen-year-old Richard Howell participated in the Greenwich Tea Burning, New Jersey&rsquo;s answer to the Boston Tea Party.2 A group of local patriots, including both Howell brothers, raided a merchant&rsquo;s cellar and burned British tea in the town square. The Howell Homestead served as a staging area. It was Richard Howell&rsquo;s first act of open defiance against the Crown.
In the years before the war he read law in Cumberland County and served on local committees of correspondence, the colonial political networks that coordinated resistance to British rule. When war came in 1776, he was ready.
Military Career (1776–1779)# Richard Howell was commissioned as an officer in the 2nd New Jersey Regiment of the Continental Army, serving under Brigadier General William Maxwell. His twin brother Lewis enlisted as Regimental Surgeon in the same regiment. See: 2nd New Jersey Regiment.3
The Major Battles# Howell saw action at three of the war&rsquo;s most significant engagements:4
Battle of Brandywine (September 11, 1777), General Howe&rsquo;s British forces outflanked Washington&rsquo;s army near Philadelphia. The Continentals suffered heavy losses but held together. Battle of Germantown (October 4, 1777), Washington&rsquo;s bold counterattack against the British occupying Philadelphia. Despite initial success, the attack was repulsed. The engagement demonstrated the Continental Army&rsquo;s growing capability. Battle of Monmouth Courthouse (June 28, 1778), One of the longest battles of the war, fought in brutal summer heat. The Continental Army, newly disciplined after Valley Forge, held its ground. The battle ended in a strategic draw. Valley Forge# The regiment wintered at Valley Forge with Washington&rsquo;s main army in 1777–1778.3 The encampment tested every man&rsquo;s endurance.
The Death of Lewis Howell# Richard&rsquo;s twin brother Dr. Lewis B. Howell fell ill with fever during the Monmouth campaign. He was sick at the time of the Battle of Monmouth (June 28, 1778) and resigned from the army on July 5, 1778. He died shortly thereafter at a tavern near Monmouth Court House. He was 23 years old. The two brothers had been inseparable, born together, raised together, gone to war together. Lewis&rsquo;s death was among the most personal losses Richard endured in the conflict.5
Intelligence Officer for General Washington# Beyond his role as a combat officer, Richard Howell served as a trusted intelligence operative for General Washington. He gathered British troop movement information from behind enemy lines and reported it through the chain of command, sometimes directly to Washington, sometimes through General Maxwell at Elizabethtown and Colonel Shreve at Newark.
His intelligence letters survive in The Papers of George Washington. Two of the most significant:
June 20, 1778, Howell&rsquo;s intelligence letter to Benedict Arnold, then Military Governor of Philadelphia, reporting on British positions and movements in the days before Monmouth. Arnold forwarded it to General Maxwell. The letter was written eight days before the battle.6 October 5, 1778, Howell&rsquo;s letter to Colonel Israel Shreve, reporting from Black Point, New Jersey, on British movements and his personal affairs.7 Washington&rsquo;s headquarters documented Howell&rsquo;s intelligence role in a series of letters between August 1778 and March 1779, see Revolutionary War Correspondence.
Resignation and Continued Service# In April 1779, Richard Howell resigned his commission from the 2nd New Jersey Regiment, citing illness in the army, family obligations, the loss of Lewis, and the need to return to law practice.5
But his service did not end there. A January 3, 1780 document from Washington&rsquo;s headquarters at Morristown records that Howell was employed by the Board of War on a secret &ldquo;Plan of Intelligence&rdquo;, a covert intelligence role that continued even after his formal resignation.8 He was, in modern terms, a case officer who had transitioned from the military to civilian intelligence work.
Statesman and Governor# Ratifying the Constitution (1787)# Richard Howell served as one of three delegates elected from Gloucester County, New Jersey, to the state convention that ratified the United States Constitution on December 18, 1787.9 He was among the men who formally brought New Jersey into the new republic.
Governor of New Jersey (1793–1801)# In 1793, Richard Howell was elected Governor of New Jersey, the third person to hold that office under the Constitution. As Governor, he was also Commander in Chief of the New Jersey militia. He was re-elected annually and served six consecutive terms, making his one of the longer governorships of the early republic.
His tenure spanned the turbulent Federalist era: the rise of partisan politics between Federalists and Democratic-Republicans, the quasi-war with France, and the political battles of the Adams administration.
The Whiskey Rebellion (1794)# The most dramatic episode of Howell&rsquo;s governorship came in 1794, when President Washington called on the states to suppress a violent uprising in western Pennsylvania against the federal whiskey excise tax.10 Governor Howell personally commanded New Jersey&rsquo;s militia contingent, the &ldquo;Jersey Blues&rdquo;, marching them west. Washington led the combined state forces, assembling the largest army since the Revolution. The rebellion collapsed without major battle, largely because of the overwhelming show of force.
Howell&rsquo;s prompt, capable response demonstrated that this former soldier had lost none of his military instincts in the governor&rsquo;s chair.
The Poet# Richard Howell was also a man of letters. He wrote patriotic songs, one of which was performed for General Washington at a reception in Trenton, New Jersey, as Washington traveled from Mount Vernon to New York City for his inauguration as the first President of the United States in April 1789. The identity of the specific song remains an open research question, though Howell&rsquo;s authorship of a welcoming ode for Washington at Trenton is documented in The Papers of George Washington, Presidential Series, vol. 2, p. 109.11
Death and Legacy# Richard Howell died on April 28, 1802, in Trenton, New Jersey, at the age of 47.12 He was buried at Friends Burying Ground, South Broad Street, Trenton, NJ.13
He left behind a wife, nine children, and a record of service that spanned nearly every chapter of the founding era, the Tea Burning, the Revolutionary War, the Constitution, and the early republic. Among his descendants, his son William B. Howell became the father of Varina Howell Davis, who married Jefferson Davis and served as First Lady of the Confederacy. He was soldier, spy, delegate, governor, and poet. And he remains, as his descendants have noted, one of America&rsquo;s often overlooked, unsung heroes.
