George Washington to Nathanael Greene, August 21, 1778# Major Richard Howell making observations concerning the British Fleet.
Source: John C. Fitzpatrick, ed., The Writings of George Washington from the Original Manuscript Sources, 1745–1799, vol. 12 (Washington, D.C.: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1934).14
White-plains, August 21, 1778.
Dear Sir: On Wednesday afternoon I received your favor of the 12th 15 and 13th Inst. by Mr. Hulet the Pilot, who did not arrive in Camp &rsquo;till then. I am much obliged by your particular relation of matters, and request that you will continue it from time to time whenever opportunity will permit.
[. . .] There is one circumstance in your relation, of which I was exceedingly sorry to hear.16 You will readily know which it is. I wish the utmost harmony to prevail as it is essential to success; and that no occasions be omitted on our part to cultivate it.
Your operations have been greatly retarded by the late violent storm; but as it is now over, I trust things will go on prosperously and that you will be rejoined by Count D&rsquo;Estaing who has been kept out so long by it. Indeed from General Sullivan&rsquo;s Letter of the 17th., I flatter myself you will have made a compleat reduction of the Enemy&rsquo;s force before this reaches you, and that the next advices I receive will announce it.
By a Letter which I received yesterday from General Maxwell, inclosing one from Major Howell,17 (who I have stationed at black point for the purpose of observn) it appears certain, that Sixteen of Lord Howe&rsquo;s fleet entered the Hook on the 17th. That on that, and the preceding day, there had been heard severe Canonades at Sea, and that it was reported in New York that a 64 Gun Ship and several Transports had been taken by the French Squadron. I wish the fact may be so as to the capture, and that the Count may be with you to give a narrative of it himself.18
